/*  The game's clock — such as it is.

    ★ It only PAUSES. Cufet has no way to ask what time it is, and that is not an oversight: a
    clock reading is nondeterministic, and this project holds the two backends to byte-for-byte
    agreement. A pause changes only WHEN output appears, never what it says, so it costs the
    oracle nothing — which is why a game can have this and cannot have a stopwatch.

    ★ It lives with the game rather than in `terminals`, because pausing is not a conversation
    with whoever is at the keyboard. If something else ever wants it, that is when it earns a
    home of its own and a name to go with it.

    ⚠ Named `pacing` rather than `clock` on purpose. A clock tells you the time; this decides how
    fast the game moves, and calling it the other thing would promise a reading it cannot give.
*/

Define object pacing with () and book:

    /// Stop for a while, so the next frame does not arrive immediately.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Milliseconds, and approximate: the operating system decides when to wake a sleeper, so
    /// a request for 50 is a floor rather than a promise. MEASURED 2026-09-20 at 642ms against a
    /// 600ms request, the difference being process overhead rather than drift.
    Bind void to nap, given (the number milliseconds):
        Pull a book on the c-language.
            Define c-language nap-support as [#if defined(_WIN32)
                #include <windows.h>
                static int cufet_pacing_nap(int ms) { if (ms > 0) Sleep((DWORD)ms); return 0; }
                #else
                #include <time.h>
                static int cufet_pacing_nap(int ms) {
                    struct timespec how_long;
                    if (ms <= 0) return 0;
                    how_long.tv_sec = ms / 1000;
                    how_long.tv_nsec = (long)(ms % 1000) * 1000000L;
                    nanosleep(&how_long, NULL);
                    return 0;
                }
                #endif].
            Define c-language number napped, given (the number wait), as [cufet_pacing_nap(the wait)].
            Cast napped on (milliseconds).
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
